    <entry>
        <title type="html"><![CDATA[OpenSpec tutorial series — Part 3: ADRs, the standing context]]></title>
        <id>https://www.conduction.nl/nl/academy/openspec-tutorial-3-adrs/</id>
        <link href="https://www.conduction.nl/nl/academy/openspec-tutorial-3-adrs/"/>
        <updated>2026-06-11T00:00:00.000Z</updated>
        <summary type="html"><![CDATA[A spec tells you what one feature must do. But "build a search box" leaves a hundred questions unanswered that have nothing to do with search. Which data layer? Vue 2 or Vue 3? Where do modals live? Which licence header? Answering those per spec would be madness, and an AI would answer them differently every time. That is what ADRs are for. They are the standing context that every spec is built on top of. This part explains what they are, how they are written, and how Conduction splits them across the fleet.]]></summary>
        <author>
            <name>Conduction</name>
            <uri>https://conduction.nl</uri>
        </author>
